/* Days 9 to 11 */
(window.DAYS = window.DAYS || []).push(
{
  n: 9, title: 'Designs I: storage systems', tagline: 'Full walkthroughs that combine everything so far: URL shortener, file storage, and a Dynamo-style key-value store.',
  goals: ['Apply the six-step method end to end on a classic problem', 'Separate metadata from blob storage and explain why', 'Describe how a Dynamo-style store partitions, replicates, repairs and detects failure', 'Narrate a design with numbers, trade-offs and failure cases'],
  plan: [{ min: 30, what: 'Lesson 1: URL shortener, step by step', to: 'l0' }, { min: 25, what: 'Lesson 2: Pastebin and Dropbox', to: 'l1' }, { min: 25, what: 'Lesson 3: distributed key-value store', to: 'l2' }, { min: 10, what: 'Quiz', to: 'quiz' }, { min: 30, what: 'Practice: design a distributed cache', to: 'practice' }],
  lessons: [
    {
      title: 'URL shortener, step by step', short: 'URL shortener', min: 30, body: [
        "Use this walkthrough as the template for every design: the order matters more than the content.",
        { h: '1. Requirements' },
        { ul: ['Functional: create a short link for a long URL; redirect from short to long; optional custom alias and expiry; basic click analytics.', 'Non-functional: redirects must be very fast and highly available (a dead link is a visible failure); creation can be slower; links must never collide; short links should be hard to enumerate.', 'Out of scope: user accounts, link editing.'] },
        { h: '2. Estimates (from Day 1)' },
        "100 million new links per month is about 40 writes/s; with 100:1 reads that is about 4,000 reads/s, peak about 12,000. At 500 bytes each, five years is about 3 TB. So: read-heavy, small data, one database node can store it but we want replicas for availability and read scale.",
        { h: '3. API' },
        { code: "POST /v1/links   { long_url, alias?, expires_at? }  ->  201 { short_url }\nGET  /{code}                                         ->  302 Location: long_url" },
        { h: '4. Generating the code' },
        "A 7-character code in base62 (0-9, a-z, A-Z) gives 62^7, about 3.5 trillion codes, ample for 6 billion links. How to produce unique ones:",
        { table: { head: ['Approach', 'Idea', 'Issue'], rows: [['Hash the URL, take 7 chars', 'MD5/SHA of the URL then truncate', 'Collisions; must check and retry; same URL always gives the same code (sometimes wanted)'], ['Counter + base62', 'Unique integer id, encoded in base62', 'Ids are guessable and sequential; need a distributed counter'], ['Pre-generated key service', 'A service fills a table with random unused codes; app servers lease a batch (say 1,000) and hand them out locally', 'Extra component, but no collisions and no per-request coordination'], ['Random + unique check', 'Generate random code, insert; retry on duplicate key', 'Simple; collision rate is tiny at this fill level']] } },
        "A good answer: a Snowflake-style or range-leased counter encoded in base62, optionally scrambled so codes are not sequential.",
        { diagram: 'url-shortener' },
        { h: '5. Data model and storage' },
        "One table `links(code PK, long_url, user_id, created_at, expires_at)`. Access is purely by key, no joins, so a key-value or sharded relational store works. Shard by code (hash). Reads are served mostly from a cache (Redis, cache-aside): the hot set is small.",
        { h: '6. Deep dives and trade-offs' },
        { ul: ['**301 vs 302:** a 301 (permanent) is cached by browsers, which reduces your load but means you never see repeat clicks and cannot change the target. Use 302 when you need analytics or edits.', '**Analytics:** do not write to the database on each click. Emit an event to a queue and aggregate asynchronously (Day 7).', '**Expiry:** lazily on read and with a background cleaner that deletes expired rows in batches.', '**Abuse:** rate limit creation, scan target URLs for malware/phishing, block enumeration with non-sequential codes.', '**Availability:** replicas across zones, cache in front, the redirect path has few dependencies, and a CDN can cache redirects for popular codes.'] },
        { say: "The read path is the one that matters: CDN or cache first, then a sharded key-value lookup, then a 302. The write path has a leased id range encoded in base62 so creation needs no cross-node coordination." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Pastebin and Dropbox: storing blobs and files', short: 'Blob and file storage', min: 25, body: [
        "**The key principle:** separate **metadata** (small, structured, queried, needs consistency) from **content** (large, opaque, immutable, needs cheap durability). Metadata lives in a database; content lives in **object storage** (S3 and its cousins), which replicates or erasure-codes data across zones for very high durability at low cost, and is served directly through presigned URLs and a CDN rather than through your app servers.",
        { h: 'Pastebin' },
        { ul: ['Create: the app generates a key (as in the URL shortener), writes the text to object storage under that key, and a metadata row (key, owner, created, expiry, size) to the database. Small pastes can live directly in the DB.', 'Read: look up metadata (cache), then return the content or a redirect to a CDN URL. Pastes are immutable, so CDN caching is trivially safe.', 'Expiry: TTL in metadata, a background job deletes expired objects, and the read path also checks expiry.'] },
        { h: 'Dropbox / Google Drive: sync files across devices' },
        "The hard part is syncing large, changing files efficiently and consistently.",
        { ol: ['**Chunking:** split each file into blocks (for example 4 MB). Compute a hash (SHA-256) per block.', '**Deduplication and delta sync:** the client asks the server which block hashes it already has, and uploads only the missing ones. Editing one paragraph of a 1 GB file uploads one 4 MB block. Identical blocks across users are stored once (content-addressed storage).', '**Metadata service:** a transactional database holds the file tree, versions, and the ordered list of block hashes per file version. This part needs strong consistency; it is small and shardable by user or namespace.', '**Block storage:** immutable blocks keyed by hash in object storage. Immutability makes caching, replication and retries easy.', '**Notifications:** clients keep a long-poll or WebSocket connection to a notification service; on change, they are told to pull the new version list. A queue decouples uploads from fan-out to other devices.', '**Conflicts:** two devices edit the same file offline. Do not silently overwrite: keep both and create a "conflicted copy", or use version numbers and let the user merge.', '**Resumable uploads:** track which blocks arrived so a dropped connection continues where it stopped.'] },
        { tip: "When a design involves big files, say immediately: 'The bytes never go through my API servers. The client uploads straight to object storage using a presigned URL, and my service only handles metadata.'" }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Designing a distributed key-value store', short: 'Key-value store', min: 25, body: [
        "This is the best revision exercise in the course: it combines partitioning, replication, quorums, failure detection and storage engines. The reference design is Amazon's Dynamo, which inspired Cassandra and Riak.",
        { h: 'Requirements' },
        "`put(key, value)` and `get(key)`; always writable even during failures (availability over strict consistency); scales by adding nodes; low and predictable latency; data of up to a few MB per value.",
        { h: 'The design, piece by piece' },
        { table: { head: ['Problem', 'Technique', 'Day'], rows: [['Spread data over many nodes, grow smoothly', 'Consistent hashing with virtual nodes', '5'], ['Survive node loss', 'Replicate each key to the next N (say 3) distinct nodes on the ring (the preference list)', '5'], ['Consistency vs latency', 'Tunable quorums: write to w nodes, read from r nodes; w + r > n gives overlap', '5'], ['A replica is down during a write', 'Sloppy quorum + **hinted handoff**: another node accepts the write temporarily and hands it back when the replica returns', '5'], ['Replicas diverge', '**Read repair** (on a read, fix stale replicas) plus background **anti-entropy** using **Merkle trees** to find differing key ranges cheaply', '8'], ['Concurrent writes to one key', 'Version with vector clocks, return siblings, let the application merge (or last-write-wins when loss is acceptable)', '5'], ['Who is alive? No central master', '**Gossip protocol**: nodes periodically exchange membership and heartbeat info with random peers; failure detected when heartbeats stop (an accrual failure detector)', '8'], ['Fast local storage', 'Write-ahead log, in-memory memtable, SSTables on disk, compaction, Bloom filters', '4']] } },
        { h: 'Request flow' },
        "A client contacts any node (or a client library that knows the ring). The **coordinator** for that key is the first node in its preference list. For a write it forwards to N replicas and returns success after w acknowledge. For a read it asks r replicas, compares versions, returns the latest (or siblings) and triggers read repair.",
        { h: 'The knobs, and what they mean' },
        { ul: ['N=3, w=1, r=1: fastest, least consistent (a write-heavy cache).', 'N=3, w=2, r=2: balanced, strongly consistent when no failures.', 'w=N: durable but one down node blocks writes. r=1 with w=N: fast reads.'] },
        { say: "I would model this on Dynamo: consistent hashing with virtual nodes, replication factor 3 across zones, tunable quorums defaulting to 2/2, hinted handoff for availability, Merkle-tree anti-entropy, gossip for membership, and an LSM storage engine per node." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Web crawler', short: 'Web crawler', deep: true, body: [
        "A crawler discovers and downloads billions of pages. The interesting parts are scale, politeness and duplicate avoidance.",
        { ul: ['**URL frontier:** the queue of URLs to fetch. It must prioritize (important, fast-changing pages first) and be **polite**: never hammer one host. A common design has many FIFO queues (one per host, mapped to a worker) behind a priority selector, with a per-host delay from `robots.txt` and the host\'s response speed.', '**Fetcher workers** (thousands, distributed by host hash so one worker owns each host) with a local **DNS cache** because resolving names is a major cost.', '**Parser and extractor:** extract text and links, normalize URLs (lowercase host, remove fragments, resolve relative paths).', '**Deduplication:** URL seen-set (a Bloom filter or sharded key-value set) so you do not fetch twice; **content fingerprints** (hashes, or simhash for near-duplicates) so you do not store mirrors.', '**Crawler traps:** infinite calendars and session-id URLs. Cap depth and pages per host, detect repeating patterns.', '**Freshness:** re-crawl pages by estimated change rate; use HTTP conditional requests (`If-Modified-Since`, ETag).', '**Storage:** raw pages in object storage, extracted data in a store feeding the indexer.'] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Counters and top-K (trending)', short: 'Counters and top-K', deep: true, body: [
        "**Counting likes or views at scale:** a single row incremented by 100,000 users per second becomes a hot key. Options: **sharded counters** (N sub-counters per item, increment a random one, sum on read); **buffer and batch** (aggregate in memory or a stream and write every second); **approximate** counts are often fine (HyperLogLog for uniques).",
        "**Top-K trending items over the last hour:** stream events through Kafka into a stream processor that keeps a **count-min sketch** or exact counters per time bucket (one-minute tumbling windows), merges the last 60 buckets with a min-heap of size K per partition, then merges partitions' heaps into a global top-K, written to a cache for reads. Trade-off: exact counts need more memory and shuffling; sketches give approximate counts with bounded error and constant memory.",
        "This pattern (events, windowed aggregation, serve from cache) answers many questions: trending hashtags, most-viewed videos, leaderboard, ad click counting."
      ]
    }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'How many unique codes can a 7-character base62 string represent?', opts: ['About 62 million', 'About 3.5 trillion', 'About 1 quadrillion', 'About 7 billion'], a: 1, why: '62^7 is about 3.5 x 10^12, comfortably more than enough for billions of links.' },
    { q: 'You want to count every click on short links. Which redirect fits?', opts: ['301 permanent', '302 temporary', '200 with a script', '404'], a: 1, why: 'Browsers cache 301s, so repeat clicks never reach you. 302 keeps every click visible.' },
    { q: 'Why separate metadata from file content in a Dropbox-like system?', opts: ['Legal reasons', 'Metadata needs consistency and queries; content is large, immutable and best in cheap durable object storage served directly', 'To avoid encryption', 'It is faster to compile'], a: 1, why: 'Different data, different needs: a transactional DB for the file tree and object storage for blocks.' },
    { q: 'A user edits one paragraph in a 1 GB document. Why does Dropbox upload only a few MB?', opts: ['Compression', 'Files are split into hashed chunks and only changed chunks are uploaded', 'Servers guess the changes', 'It uploads at night'], a: 1, why: 'Chunk hashing enables delta sync and deduplication.' },
    { q: 'In a Dynamo-style store, what is hinted handoff for?', opts: ['Compacting files', 'Accepting writes for a down replica on another node and delivering them back later', 'Encrypting data', 'Electing a leader'], a: 1, why: 'It keeps writes available during node failures; the temporary holder forwards the data when the real replica returns.' },
    { q: 'What do Merkle trees do in anti-entropy repair?', opts: ['Compress values', 'Let two replicas quickly find which key ranges differ by comparing hashes top-down', 'Order writes', 'Detect failures'], a: 1, why: 'If root hashes match the replicas agree; otherwise descend only into differing subtrees, avoiding a full comparison.' }
  ],
  practice: {
    min: 30,
    prompt: ["**Design a distributed in-memory cache** (think Memcached or a Redis cluster) used by hundreds of services. 1 million operations per second in total, 10 TB of cached data, p99 latency under 5 ms. Services must keep working if a cache node dies.", "Walk through the method: requirements, numbers, API, architecture, then deep-dive on partitioning, replication, eviction and hot keys. Say what consistency guarantees you give."],
    hints: ['How many nodes does 10 TB need if a node has 64 GB of RAM? How many operations per second per node?', 'Client-side or proxy-side routing?', 'Does a cache need replication? What does losing a node cost?'],
    outline: [
      { ul: ['**Requirements:** get, set with TTL, delete; no durability required; eventual consistency acceptable; cache invalidation is the client\'s job; multi-tenant.', '**Numbers:** 10 TB / 64 GB is about 160 nodes (use about 200 for headroom); 1M ops/s / 200 = 5,000 ops/s per node, comfortable (Redis does about 100K/s).', '**Architecture:** clients (or a thin proxy such as Twemproxy/Envoy) hold the ring and route by key; consistent hashing with virtual nodes; a config/membership service (etcd or gossip) distributes the node list.', '**Failure:** when a node dies, its keys miss and reload from the source of truth. Replication optional: 1 replica per shard trades memory for smoother failover. Use replicas for hot or expensive-to-rebuild data.', '**Eviction:** per-node LRU with maxmemory plus TTL with jitter.', '**Hot keys:** client-side local cache for very hot keys, replicate the hot key across several nodes, detect hot keys by sampling counters.', '**Protecting the database:** single-flight on misses, TTL jitter, circuit breakers.', '**Scaling:** add nodes; consistent hashing moves 1/N of keys; warm-up; avoid thundering herd on resize.', '**Operations:** metrics for hit ratio, evictions, memory, per-key hot spots, latency percentiles.'] }
    ],
    rubric: 'Check: sizing math (nodes from memory and ops/s), consistent hashing with vnodes, client vs proxy routing and membership distribution, failure behaviour (misses reload, optional replicas), eviction + TTL jitter, hot key mitigations, stampede protection, stated consistency guarantee, and operational metrics.'
  },
  cheat: ['Template: requirements, numbers, API, data model, high-level design, deep dive, bottlenecks.', 'URL shortener: base62 ids from a leased counter, key-value store, cache in front, 302 for analytics, events to a queue.', 'Metadata in a database; blobs in object storage via presigned URLs and CDN. Chunk + hash gives dedupe and delta sync.', 'Dynamo-style KV: consistent hashing + vnodes, N replicas, w/r quorums, hinted handoff, read repair, Merkle trees, gossip, LSM engine.', 'Crawler: polite per-host queues, DNS cache, Bloom filter for seen URLs, content fingerprints.', 'Hot counter: shard it or batch it.'],
  cards: [
    ['How many codes in 7 chars of base62?', 'About 3.5 trillion (62^7).'],
    ['301 vs 302 for a shortener?', '301 is cached by browsers (less load, no analytics, cannot edit). 302 sends every click through you.'],
    ['Why use a leased range or Snowflake-style id for codes?', 'Unique ids without per-request coordination; encode in base62; scramble to avoid guessable sequences.'],
    ['How do you store big files?', 'Object storage with presigned URL uploads and CDN delivery; metadata in a database.'],
    ['What makes Dropbox sync efficient?', 'Chunking, per-chunk hashes, uploading only missing chunks, content-addressed dedupe.'],
    ['Name the parts of a Dynamo-style store.', 'Consistent hashing with vnodes, replication N, tunable quorums, hinted handoff, read repair, Merkle trees, gossip, vector clocks, LSM storage.'],
    ['What is a sloppy quorum?', 'Writes may go to the first N healthy nodes rather than the designated ones, keeping availability; hinted handoff later returns data to the right replica.'],
    ['What does gossip do?', 'Nodes periodically exchange membership and health info with random peers so every node learns of joins and failures without a central master.'],
    ['How does a crawler stay polite?', 'Per-host queues with delays, robots.txt rules, adaptive rate based on server response.'],
    ['How do you count a very hot item?', 'Sharded counters summed on read, or buffered/batched writes; approximate with sketches if exact is not required.']
  ]
},

{
  n: 10, title: 'Designs II: feeds, chat, notifications', tagline: 'Real-time and fan-out systems: where caching, queues, partitioning and push meet.',
  goals: ['Choose fan-out on write, read or hybrid for a feed and justify it', 'Design a chat system with message routing, ordering, presence and offline delivery', 'Design a notification pipeline with priorities, dedupe and provider failover', 'Sketch proximity search, video streaming and search at a high level'],
  plan: [{ min: 30, what: 'Lesson 1: news feed', to: 'l0' }, { min: 30, what: 'Lesson 2: chat system', to: 'l1' }, { min: 20, what: 'Lesson 3: notification system', to: 'l2' }, { min: 10, what: 'Quiz', to: 'quiz' }, { min: 30, what: 'Practice: notification pipeline', to: 'practice' }],
  lessons: [
    {
      title: 'News feed', short: 'News feed', min: 30, body: [
        "**Problem:** show each user a ranked list of recent posts from people they follow, quickly, for hundreds of millions of users. Reads dwarf writes (100:1 or more).",
        { h: 'Requirements and numbers' },
        "300 million daily users opening the feed 10 times a day is 3 billion feed loads, about 35,000 reads/s average, 100,000+ at peak. Posts: 100 million per day, about 1,200 writes/s. A user follows about 200 accounts on average; a celebrity has 50 million followers.",
        { h: 'Two ways to build a feed' },
        { table: { head: ['', 'Fan-out on write (push)', 'Fan-out on read (pull)'], rows: [['How', 'On post, copy the post id into each follower\'s feed list', 'On feed load, fetch recent posts from each followee and merge'], ['Read cost', 'One cheap list read', 'Expensive: query 200 sources, merge, sort'], ['Write cost', 'Proportional to follower count (50M for a celebrity)', 'Just one write'], ['Staleness', 'Feed is precomputed, fast, mostly fresh', 'Always fresh'], ['Weakness', 'Celebrity posts cause a write storm and waste on inactive followers', 'Slow reads at scale']] } },
        { diagram: 'feed-fanout' },
        { h: 'The hybrid used in practice' },
        { ul: ['Push for normal users: workers write the post id into followers\' feed lists in Redis (a capped list of the latest few hundred ids), only for **active** users.', 'Pull for celebrities: do not fan out; at read time merge the precomputed feed with recent posts from the celebrities this user follows.', 'Cold or returning users: build the feed on demand and cache it.'] },
        { h: 'Putting the pieces together' },
        { ul: ['**Post service** stores posts (sharded by author or post id with time-ordered ids). **Graph service** stores follow relationships both ways (followers of X, accounts X follows), each sharded by its first key.', '**Fan-out service** consumes a post event queue, looks up followers in pages, and writes ids to feed caches, idempotently.', '**Feed service** reads the id list, hydrates ids into full posts (batch get from a post cache), applies **ranking** (relevance model, filtered by blocks and privacy) and returns a page.', '**Pagination** uses a cursor (last post id or score) so the feed is stable while new posts arrive. **Media** goes to object storage and a CDN.', '**Failure and consistency:** eventual consistency is fine (a post appearing seconds late is invisible); deletes must propagate (filter at read time against a tombstone check).'] },
        { tip: "Call out the celebrity problem unprompted and propose the hybrid. It is the single most expected insight in this question." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Chat system', short: 'Chat', min: 30, body: [
        "**Problem:** deliver messages between users in real time, in order, reliably, including when recipients are offline, with group chats and presence.",
        { diagram: 'chat-system' },
        { h: 'Core design' },
        { ul: ['**Connections:** each client keeps a WebSocket to a **gateway** server. Gateways are stateful, so a **session map** (user id to gateway id, in Redis with a TTL refreshed by heartbeats) tells the chat service where each user is connected.', '**Send path:** gateway forwards to the **chat service**, which assigns an ordered id, **persists first**, then looks up recipients\' gateways and pushes. If a recipient is offline, only the stored copy exists and a push notification is sent.', '**Client acks:** the client confirms receipt, producing delivered and read states. The server retries unacknowledged messages and the client dedupes by message id.', '**Reconnect and sync:** a client reconnecting sends its last seen message id per conversation and receives everything after it. This one mechanism covers offline users, flaky mobile networks and multiple devices.'] },
        { h: 'Storage and ordering' },
        "Messages are append-heavy, read by conversation, newest first. Use a store partitioned by **conversation id**, clustered by time-ordered message id (a wide-column store like Cassandra, or sharded SQL). Ordering within a conversation comes from a per-conversation sequence assigned by whichever node owns that conversation, or from time-ordered ids from a Snowflake-like generator; global ordering across conversations is unnecessary.",
        { h: 'Groups' },
        "For small groups, fan out on write: store once, deliver to each member's gateway. For huge groups or channels (thousands to millions) switch to a pull-based model similar to the celebrity feed: members fetch new messages on demand and receive only a lightweight 'new message' signal.",
        { h: 'Presence' },
        "Each gateway heartbeats user status into Redis with a short TTL; 'online' means the key exists. Do not broadcast every status flip to all contacts (a storm): send presence changes only to users who are currently viewing the contact, or poll on demand.",
        { h: 'Other points' },
        { ul: ['End-to-end encryption means servers store ciphertext and key exchange happens between devices; it constrains server-side features like search.', 'Media goes to object storage; the message carries only a reference.', 'Rate limit senders and scan for abuse where the product allows.', 'Gateways scale horizontally; user to gateway routing rides on pub/sub (a channel per gateway or per user).'] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Notification system', short: 'Notifications', min: 20, body: [
        "**Problem:** send push, email, SMS and in-app notifications triggered by many services, reliably and without spamming users. Messages range from critical (a login code that expires in 60 seconds) to bulk marketing (millions at once).",
        { h: 'Pipeline' },
        { ol: ['**Producers** (services) call a notification API or publish an event with: user id, template id, parameters, priority, an **idempotency key**.', '**Notification service** validates, checks **user preferences** (opt-outs, channels, quiet hours, frequency caps), renders the template (localized), and enqueues one job per channel.', '**Separate queues by priority and channel** (OTP, transactional, marketing × push, email, SMS). A marketing blast of 10 million must never delay a login code, so isolated queues and worker pools are essential.', '**Channel workers** call the providers (APNs and FCM for push, an email provider, an SMS gateway) with **retries, exponential backoff and a dead-letter queue**. Fail over to a second provider when the first is degraded.', '**Result tracking:** delivery receipts and bounces come back via webhooks into a store; invalid device tokens are pruned; metrics power dashboards.'] },
        { h: 'Reliability and fairness' },
        { ul: ['At-least-once delivery from queues means duplicates: dedupe on the idempotency key before sending so a user does not get the same OTP twice.', '**Rate limiting** per user (no more than N marketing messages a day) and per provider (respect their quotas, smooth bursts through the queue).', '**Scheduling:** delayed jobs for reminders, and time zone aware sends. Use a delay queue or a scheduler that scans due jobs.', '**Bulk sends:** break a campaign into chunks of recipients fed progressively, not one 10-million-item message.', '**Scale numbers:** 50 million notifications a day is about 600/s average, with spikes of 20x for campaigns. Easy to size once you separate queues.'] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Proximity search (Uber, Yelp)', short: 'Proximity search', deep: true, body: [
        "**Problem:** find nearby drivers or restaurants quickly given a latitude and longitude. A naive query `WHERE distance(lat, lng) < 2 km` scans everything.",
        { ul: ['**Geohash:** encodes a location into a string where a longer common prefix means closer (usually). Divide the world into cells; index by cell. To search near a point, query its cell and the 8 neighbours, because a point near a cell edge has neighbours in the adjacent cell (the boundary problem), then filter by exact distance.', '**Quadtree:** recursively splits dense areas into four, adapting to density (a city centre has small cells, an ocean has one large). Kept in memory.', '**S2 / H3:** hierarchical cell systems (Google S2, Uber H3 hexagons) with better uniformity; used in production.'] },
        "For moving objects (drivers update every 4 seconds, say 500,000 drivers is 125,000 writes/s) keep the live index in memory, in Redis geo structures or purpose-built services, sharded by region or cell prefix, and treat location data as ephemeral (a lost update is replaced in seconds). Persist only trips. A matching service queries nearby drivers, ranks by ETA and availability, and offers the ride using an atomic claim so two riders cannot get the same driver."
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Video streaming (YouTube, Netflix)', short: 'Video streaming', deep: true, body: [
        "**Cost model:** storage and especially bandwidth dominate. Everything is built around delivering bytes cheaply from close to the viewer.",
        { ol: ['**Upload:** resumable chunked upload straight to object storage.', '**Transcode:** a pipeline (a DAG of tasks on a queue) converts the original into many renditions (240p to 4K) and codecs, in parallel segments, plus thumbnails and audio tracks. CPU heavy, autoscaled, retried safely (Day 7).', '**Segment and package:** each rendition is cut into small segments (2 to 10 s) described by a manifest, as in HLS or MPEG-DASH.', '**Adaptive bitrate (ABR):** the player measures bandwidth and picks the best rendition segment by segment, switching up or down seamlessly. This is why streams adapt rather than stall.', '**Delivery via CDN:** segments are plain files, ideal for CDN caching. Netflix places appliances inside ISP networks (Open Connect) so most traffic never crosses the wider internet. Popular videos are cached everywhere; the long tail is served from origin or regional caches.', '**Metadata** (titles, comments, view counts) lives in ordinary databases and caches; recommendations and search are separate systems fed by events.'] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Search and typeahead backend', short: 'Search backend', deep: true, body: [
        "A **search engine** is built on an **inverted index**: for each term, a sorted list of the documents that contain it (a postings list), with positions and frequencies. A query for 'red shoes' intersects the lists for 'red' and 'shoes', then **ranks** results (TF-IDF or BM25 scoring, then learned ranking and personalization). Text is tokenized, lower-cased, stemmed and stripped of stop words first.",
        { ul: ['**Scaling:** shard the index by document (each shard indexes a subset and answers any query over its subset); a query is scattered to all shards, each returns its top results, and a coordinator merges them. Replicate shards for availability and read throughput. Elasticsearch and Solr work this way.', '**Freshness:** new documents go into small in-memory segments that are searchable within a second and merged into larger segments in the background (a design similar to LSM trees).', '**Source of truth:** keep the primary database authoritative and feed the search index asynchronously (events or change data capture); a search index can always be rebuilt.', '**Typeahead:** store popular queries in a **trie** (prefix tree) where each node caches its top-K completions, rebuilt offline from logs on a schedule (hourly or daily) and served from memory or cache; the client debounces keystrokes (Day 13).'] }
      ]
    }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why is pure fan-out on write a problem for a celebrity with 50 million followers?', opts: ['Reads become slow', 'One post triggers 50 million writes, creating a write storm and delay', 'It breaks ordering', 'It requires strong consistency'], a: 1, why: 'Push cost scales with follower count. Hybrid designs skip fan-out for celebrities and merge their posts at read time.' },
    { q: 'In a chat system, how does a reconnecting client get messages it missed?', opts: ['The server resends everything', 'It sends its last seen message id and the server returns everything after it', 'It cannot', 'It re-registers'], a: 1, why: 'A per-conversation cursor handles offline users, flaky networks and multiple devices uniformly.' },
    { q: 'Why persist a chat message before attempting delivery?', opts: ['To save bandwidth', 'So the message is not lost if delivery or the recipient\'s gateway fails', 'To encrypt it', 'To reduce latency'], a: 1, why: 'Durability first, delivery second: the stored copy lets you retry and sync later.' },
    { q: 'A marketing campaign to 10M users must not delay one-time passwords. What do you do?', opts: ['Send OTPs first manually', 'Use separate priority queues and worker pools per priority and channel', 'Increase timeouts', 'Limit campaigns to 1M'], a: 1, why: 'Isolation (bulkheading) means a flood in one queue cannot starve another.' },
    { q: 'When searching nearby points with geohash, why query the 8 neighbouring cells too?', opts: ['To increase results', 'Nearby points can lie just across a cell boundary and have a different prefix', 'Geohash is random', 'Cells overlap'], a: 1, why: 'The boundary problem: closeness does not guarantee the same cell. Check neighbours and filter by true distance.' },
    { q: 'What lets a video player avoid stalling when bandwidth drops?', opts: ['Larger buffers only', 'Adaptive bitrate: short segments in multiple renditions, switching per segment', 'Using UDP', 'Re-uploading'], a: 1, why: 'The manifest lists several renditions; the player picks per segment according to measured throughput.' }
  ],
  practice: {
    min: 30,
    prompt: ["**Design a notification system** for an app with 50 million users. It sends: login codes (must arrive within 10 seconds), order updates, and marketing blasts to up to 10 million users. Channels: push, email, SMS. Users can opt out per channel and per category. Providers are unreliable and rate limited.", "Cover: API, pipeline stages, queues, retries, dedupe, user preferences, rate limits, provider failover and how you prove a notification was delivered."],
    hints: ['What goes wrong if all notifications share one queue?', 'Where do you check preferences, and what about a user changing preferences after the job is queued?', 'What is your idempotency key?'],
    outline: [
      { ol: ['**API/event:** (user_id, category, template, params, priority, idempotency_key). Validate, authenticate producers, return 202.', '**Preferences and rules service:** opt-outs, quiet hours, frequency caps; check at enqueue time and again just before send (preferences can change).', '**Templating/localization,** then one job per channel onto **priority x channel queues**: critical (OTP) with dedicated workers and no rate limit beyond provider limits, transactional, marketing (throttled).', '**Workers** call providers with timeouts and backoff retries; dead-letter after N attempts; circuit breaker per provider with automatic failover to a secondary (SMS vendor B).', '**Dedupe:** unique constraint on idempotency key in a store with TTL; consumers idempotent.', '**Bulk campaigns:** expand audience in pages into batches, enqueue progressively, pace per provider quota; a campaign can be paused.', '**Tracking:** provider webhooks for delivered/bounced/opened into a status table and analytics stream; prune invalid tokens; dashboards and alerts on queue age and failure rate; OTP delivery latency SLO (p99 < 10 s).', '**Scale:** 50M/day is about 600/s average; plan for 20x bursts via queue buffering and autoscaled workers.'] }
    ],
    rubric: 'Look for priority/channel queue isolation, preference checks (twice), idempotency keys and dedupe, retries with backoff and DLQ, provider failover/circuit breaker, batched paced campaigns, delivery tracking via webhooks, rate limiting per user and per provider, and specific SLO for OTP.'
  },
  cheat: ['Feed: hybrid. Push for normal users into per-user id lists, pull for celebrities at read time. Cursor pagination. Hydrate ids from cache.', 'Chat: WebSocket gateways + session map + chat service; persist first, then deliver; client acks; reconnect with last seen id; per-conversation ordering; groups push small, pull large.', 'Presence: heartbeat with TTL in Redis; avoid broadcasting every change.', 'Notifications: priority x channel queues, preferences, idempotency, retries, provider failover, per-user and provider rate limits.', 'Proximity: geohash/quadtree/S2 plus neighbour cells; live locations in memory.', 'Video: transcode to renditions, HLS/DASH segments, ABR, CDN everywhere. Search: inverted index sharded by document, scatter-gather.'],
  cards: [
    ['Fan-out on write vs on read?', 'Write: precompute each follower\'s feed (fast reads, expensive writes for big accounts). Read: assemble on request (cheap writes, slow reads). Hybrid in practice.'],
    ['How do you solve the celebrity problem?', 'Do not fan out their posts; merge their recent posts into followers\' feeds at read time.'],
    ['How does a chat server know where a user is connected?', 'A session map (user id to gateway) in Redis, refreshed by heartbeats with TTL.'],
    ['Why persist before delivering a chat message?', 'So failures during delivery do not lose the message; allows retry and sync.'],
    ['How do offline or multi-device users catch up?', 'Send last seen message id; server returns everything after it.'],
    ['How do you handle very large group chats?', 'Pull-based: members fetch on demand after a lightweight signal rather than fan-out per message.'],
    ['Why separate queues per priority in notifications?', 'So bulk sends cannot delay critical messages like OTPs (isolation/bulkhead).'],
    ['What is geohash and its pitfall?', 'String encoding of location where prefix indicates proximity; boundary problem means neighbours must be searched.'],
    ['What is adaptive bitrate streaming?', 'Video is stored as short segments in several qualities; the player selects each segment\'s quality by measured bandwidth.'],
    ['What is an inverted index?', 'Mapping from term to the documents (postings) containing it, used to answer text queries and rank results.']
  ]
},

{
  n: 11, title: 'Frontend I: rendering and performance', tagline: 'How the browser turns code into pixels, and how to choose a rendering strategy and make pages fast.',
  goals: ['Explain the critical rendering path and why some things block it', 'Choose between CSR, SSR, SSG, ISR and streaming for a page', 'Name and improve the Core Web Vitals', 'Diagnose a slow page methodically'],
  plan: [{ min: 25, what: 'Lesson 1: browser rendering pipeline', to: 'l0' }, { min: 25, what: 'Lesson 2: rendering strategies', to: 'l1' }, { min: 30, what: 'Lesson 3: performance toolbox', to: 'l2' }, { min: 10, what: 'Lab: waterfall', to: 'lab' }, { min: 10, what: 'Quiz', to: 'quiz' }, { min: 20, what: 'Practice: fix a slow product page', to: 'practice' }],
  lessons: [
    {
      title: 'From bytes to pixels: the browser pipeline', short: 'Rendering pipeline', min: 25, body: [
        "A frontend system design answer is credible only if you understand what the browser does with your code. The path from HTML to pixels is called the **critical rendering path**.",
        { ol: ['**Parse HTML** into the **DOM** tree. Parsing is incremental; the browser starts work before the whole file arrives.', '**Parse CSS** into the **CSSOM**. CSS is **render-blocking**: the browser will not paint until the CSS it needs is loaded, to avoid a flash of unstyled content.', '**JavaScript** is **parser-blocking** by default: a plain `<script>` stops HTML parsing while it downloads and runs, because it might change the document. Use `defer` (download in parallel, run after parsing, in order) or `async` (run whenever ready) or `type="module"` (deferred).', '**Render tree:** DOM + CSSOM, only visible nodes.', '**Layout (reflow):** compute size and position of each box.', '**Paint:** draw pixels (text, colours, borders, shadows) into layers.', '**Composite:** the GPU combines layers into the final image.'] },
        { h: 'The main thread is the scarce resource' },
        "JavaScript, style calculation, layout and painting share a single **main thread**. While a long task runs, the page cannot respond to clicks or scroll smoothly. A **long task** is anything over 50 ms. For 60 frames per second each frame has a budget of about 16.7 ms.",
        { h: 'Cheap vs expensive changes' },
        { table: { head: ['Change', 'What the browser redoes', 'Cost'], rows: [['`transform`, `opacity` (animate these)', 'Composite only, can run on the GPU', 'Cheap, smooth'], ['`color`, `background-color`', 'Paint and composite', 'Moderate'], ['`width`, `height`, `top`, `left`, `margin`, font size', 'Layout, paint and composite for affected subtree', 'Expensive']] } },
        { warn: "Layout thrashing: alternating reads of layout (`el.offsetHeight`) and writes (`el.style.height = ...`) in a loop forces the browser to recompute layout every iteration. Batch all reads first, then all writes, or use `requestAnimationFrame`." },
        { h: 'The event loop in one paragraph' },
        "JavaScript runs one task at a time from a task queue. After each task the browser may render. **Microtasks** (promise callbacks) run right after the current task and before rendering, so an endless chain of them starves painting. `setTimeout`, events and network callbacks are separate tasks. Break heavy work into chunks (`setTimeout(0)`, `scheduler.postTask`, `requestIdleCallback`) or move it to a **Web Worker**, which runs on another thread and cannot touch the DOM."
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Rendering strategies: CSR, SSR, SSG, ISR', short: 'CSR vs SSR vs SSG', min: 25, body: [
        "**Where and when is the HTML produced?** That single question defines the strategy, and it is the most common frontend design decision.",
        { table: { head: ['Strategy', 'HTML produced', 'Strengths', 'Weaknesses', 'Good for'], rows: [['CSR (client-side rendering)', 'In the browser, from JS after load; the server sends an empty shell', 'Cheap hosting, rich interactivity after load, easy app-like navigation', 'Blank screen until JS loads and runs, slow first paint, weaker SEO and sharing previews', 'Logged-in dashboards, internal tools'], ['SSR (server-side rendering)', 'On the server, per request', 'Fast first paint with real content, SEO friendly, always fresh', 'Server cost and TTFB (time to first byte) depend on data fetching; needs hydration', 'Personalized or frequently changing public pages: e-commerce, social'], ['SSG (static site generation)', 'At build time, once', 'Fastest, served from a CDN, cheapest and most resilient', 'Content only updates on rebuild; not for per-user data', 'Docs, blogs, marketing pages'], ['ISR (incremental static regeneration)', 'Static, regenerated in the background after a TTL or on demand', 'Static speed with bounded staleness; scales to millions of pages', 'A visitor may briefly see slightly old content', 'Product catalogues, news archives'], ['Streaming SSR / islands', 'Server streams HTML in chunks; only interactive islands ship JS', 'Earlier first paint; less JS shipped', 'More framework complexity', 'Content-heavy pages with a few interactive parts']] } },
        { diagram: 'ssr-hydration' },
        { h: 'Hydration and its cost' },
        "Server-rendered HTML is visible but inert. **Hydration** downloads the JS, rebuilds the component tree in memory and attaches event handlers. During the gap the page looks ready but ignores taps, a frustrating period that hurts INP. Remedies: send less JavaScript (code splitting), **partial or selective hydration**, **server components** (components that run only on the server and ship no JS), and **islands** (hydrate only the interactive widgets).",
        { h: 'How to decide' },
        { ol: ['Is the content the same for every user and rarely changing? Static (SSG), put it on a CDN.', 'Same for everyone but changes often or has too many pages to rebuild? ISR or SSR behind a CDN with short caching.', 'Personalized after login and SEO does not matter? CSR is fine, and the simplest.', 'Personalized and SEO or first-paint critical? SSR for the shell and public data, then fetch personalized parts on the client.'] },
        { say: "Product pages are public and SEO-critical, so I would use ISR with a short revalidation, served from the CDN, and fetch the personalized bits (cart, recommendations) client-side after hydration." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Performance: Core Web Vitals and the toolbox', short: 'Performance', min: 30, body: [
        "Performance is a user-experience requirement with numbers. Google's **Core Web Vitals** are the standard ones, measured at the 75th percentile of real users:",
        { table: { head: ['Metric', 'Measures', 'Good', 'Typical causes of bad scores'], rows: [['LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)', 'When the main content (hero image or headline) appears', 'under 2.5 s', 'Slow server, render-blocking CSS/JS, huge unoptimized hero image, client-only rendering'], ['INP (Interaction to Next Paint)', 'How quickly the page responds visually to taps and clicks, worst of the session', 'under 200 ms', 'Long JS tasks, heavy hydration, big re-renders'], ['CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)', 'How much content jumps around', 'under 0.1', 'Images and ads without dimensions, late-loading fonts, injected banners']] } },
        "Also watch **TTFB** (server and network speed) and **TTI**-like measures. Measure with **lab tools** (Lighthouse, WebPageTest: reproducible) and **real user monitoring** (RUM: field data from actual devices and networks, the truth). A median desktop on fibre is not your user; test on a mid-range phone with throttled network.",
        { h: 'The toolbox, by what it attacks' },
        { table: { head: ['Problem', 'Tools'], rows: [['Too much JavaScript', 'Code splitting per route and per heavy component (dynamic `import()`), tree shaking, remove or replace big dependencies, bundle analysis, modern syntax for modern browsers, defer third-party scripts'], ['Slow network', 'Brotli/gzip compression, HTTP/2 or 3, CDN, long-lived immutable caching for hashed assets, `preconnect` to critical origins, `preload` for the LCP image and key fonts, `prefetch` the next likely page'], ['Heavy images (often the largest bytes)', 'Modern formats (AVIF, WebP), responsive `srcset` and `sizes`, correct dimensions, `loading="lazy"` below the fold (never for the hero), CDN image resizing'], ['Fonts', 'Subset, self-host, `font-display: swap`, preload the primary font, metric-matched fallbacks to avoid shift'], ['Render blocking', 'Inline critical CSS, defer the rest; `defer`/`async` scripts'], ['Main thread jank', 'Break up long tasks, memoize expensive work, virtualize long lists (render only visible rows), move computation to Web Workers, debounce handlers, animate transform/opacity'], ['Repeat visits', 'HTTP cache headers, service worker caching, stale-while-revalidate data fetching'], ['Layout shift', 'Reserve space (width/height or aspect-ratio), avoid inserting content above existing content']] } },
        { h: 'A method for any slow page' },
        { ol: ['**Measure first** with RUM and a throttled lab run; find which metric is bad and for which segment (mobile, a country).', 'Read the **waterfall**: what is on the critical path to the LCP element, and what blocks it?', 'Fix the **biggest bottleneck**, re-measure, repeat.', 'Set a **performance budget** (for example under 170 KB of JS per route) enforced in CI so it does not regress.'] },
        { tip: "Always tie an optimization to a metric: 'Lazy-loading the below-fold images and splitting the checkout bundle should cut initial JS by about 200 KB, improving LCP and INP on mid-range phones.'" }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Browser storage and service workers', short: 'Storage and offline', deep: true, body: [
        { table: { head: ['Storage', 'Capacity and lifetime', 'Notes'], rows: [['Cookies', 'About 4 KB each, sent with every request, optional expiry', 'Use `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` for session ids; they travel to the server automatically'], ['localStorage', 'About 5 MB, persistent, synchronous', 'Readable by any script on the origin, so vulnerable to XSS; blocks the main thread; strings only'], ['sessionStorage', 'About 5 MB, per tab', 'Cleared when the tab closes'], ['IndexedDB', 'Hundreds of MB or more, asynchronous, structured data', 'For offline apps, caches, queues of pending changes'], ['Cache API', 'Request-to-response store used by service workers', 'Offline assets and API responses']] } },
        { h: 'Service workers' },
        "A service worker is a script that sits between your page and the network, running in the background. It can intercept every request and decide: serve from cache, go to the network, or both. Strategies: **cache-first** (static assets: instant), **network-first** (fresh data, fall back to cache offline), **stale-while-revalidate** (show the cached version now, update it in the background). They enable offline use, instant repeat loads, background sync of queued changes, and push notifications; together these make a **PWA**. The risk is stale code stuck in users' caches, so version carefully and provide an update flow."
      ]
    },
    {
      title: 'Component rendering and re-render control', short: 'Re-render control', deep: true, body: [
        "Modern UI frameworks (React, Vue, Svelte, Solid) turn state into UI. Their performance traps are similar. This uses React terms, but the ideas generalize.",
        { ul: ['**Rendering** = running the component function to describe the UI; **reconciliation** compares the new description with the previous one (the virtual DOM diff) and updates only changed real DOM nodes. A re-render is not necessarily a DOM update, but it still costs CPU.', 'A component re-renders when its **state changes**, its **props change** (parent re-rendered), or a **context** it reads changes. A parent re-render re-renders all children by default.', '**Keys** identify list items across renders. Use stable ids, never array indexes for lists that reorder, or state will attach to the wrong rows.', '**Memoization** (`React.memo`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`) skips work when inputs are unchanged. It is not free (comparison cost, complexity) and breaks if you pass fresh object or function identities each render. Measure before adding it.', '**Structure beats memoization:** keep state as low in the tree as possible (colocate), pass children as props so changes do not re-render them, and split large contexts so a change touches only consumers that need it.', '**Concurrent rendering** (transitions, deferred values, Suspense) lets the framework keep input responsive by interrupting low-priority rendering.', '**Long lists:** virtualize. Rendering 10,000 rows is slow in any framework.'] },
        "Common interview prompt: 'typing in a search box makes the whole page laggy.' Look for: state held too high, an expensive child re-rendering on every keystroke, a missing debounce, unvirtualized lists, a context update fan-out."
      ]
    }
  ],
  lab: { title: 'Where does the time go?', min: 10, body: ["Compare how soon users see content and can interact under each rendering strategy. Change the network, round-trip time and bundle size:", { widget: 'waterfall' }, { ul: ['Set the network to Slow 3G and the bundle to 1,500 KB. Which strategy suffers most, and why?', 'Now drop the bundle to 150 KB. How does the CSR gap change?', 'Why does SSG on a CDN win even on a high round-trip time?'] }] },
  quiz: [
    { q: 'A plain `<script src="a.js">` in the head (no attributes). What happens?', opts: ['Loads in parallel without effect', 'HTML parsing pauses while it downloads and runs', 'It runs after the page loads', 'It is ignored'], a: 1, why: 'Classic scripts are parser-blocking. Use `defer` or `async` (or modules) so parsing and downloading overlap.' },
    { q: 'Which animation property is cheapest because it can be composited on the GPU?', opts: ['width', 'top', 'transform', 'margin'], a: 2, why: 'transform and opacity avoid layout and paint, so animations stay smooth even when the main thread is busy.' },
    { q: 'A public blog with rarely changing posts and huge traffic. Best strategy?', opts: ['CSR', 'SSR on every request', 'SSG (or ISR) served from a CDN', 'Client polling'], a: 2, why: 'Content is identical for all visitors and rarely changes, so prebuilt pages on a CDN are fastest and cheapest.' },
    { q: 'What does hydration do?', opts: ['Compresses HTML', 'Attaches JS and event handlers to server-rendered HTML to make it interactive', 'Fetches data', 'Warms CDN caches'], a: 1, why: 'Until hydration finishes the page is visible but not interactive, which can hurt INP.' },
    { q: 'Which metric measures visual stability?', opts: ['LCP', 'INP', 'CLS', 'TTFB'], a: 2, why: 'Cumulative Layout Shift quantifies unexpected movement of content. Reserve space for images, ads and fonts.' },
    { q: 'You see a page where each loop iteration reads `offsetHeight` then sets a style. What is the problem?', opts: ['Memory leak', 'Layout thrashing: forced synchronous layouts on each iteration', 'Event loop starvation', 'CORS'], a: 1, why: 'Interleaving reads and writes forces the browser to recompute layout every time. Batch reads, then writes.' }
  ],
  practice: {
    min: 20,
    prompt: ["**Fix a slow product page.** The product detail page on a mobile network has LCP 5.5 s, INP 450 ms and CLS 0.3. It is a client-rendered single-page app: a 1.8 MB JavaScript bundle, a 2 MB hero image, three third-party widgets (chat, analytics, reviews), web fonts, and an API call that returns product data after the bundle runs.", "Describe your diagnosis process, then list the changes in priority order with the metric each one improves and the trade-off it introduces."],
    hints: ['What is the LCP element, and what must complete before it can render?', 'Which of these block the main thread (INP)?', 'What causes layout shift here?'],
    outline: [
      { ol: ['**Diagnose:** field data (RUM) for mobile; Lighthouse/WebPageTest with throttling; read the waterfall and the LCP element (hero image) and the long tasks in the performance profile.', '**LCP:** change to SSR or ISR so HTML contains product content and the hero `<img>`; preload the hero image, serve AVIF/WebP at the right size via `srcset`, put it on a CDN, never lazy-load it. Trade-off: server/ISR complexity and cache invalidation.', '**JS weight and INP:** route-level code splitting, tree-shake, replace heavy libraries, ship less hydration (islands or server components); load chat and reviews after interaction or when idle (`defer`, dynamic import), third-party scripts via a facade; break long tasks; virtualize long review lists. Trade-off: delayed availability of secondary features.', '**CLS:** explicit width and height or aspect-ratio on images and ad slots; reserve space for the review widget; `font-display: swap` with metric-matched fallback; no inserted banners above content.', '**Network:** Brotli, HTTP/2 or 3, long-lived immutable caching for hashed assets, `preconnect` to the API and image CDN.', '**Prevent regressions:** performance budget in CI, RUM dashboards with alerts per metric.'] }
    ],
    rubric: 'A strong answer starts with measurement, identifies the LCP element, moves to SSR/ISR with an optimized preloaded hero image, cuts and defers JavaScript and third parties, fixes layout shift with reserved space and font handling, improves caching/compression, orders fixes by impact, names trade-offs, and adds budgets and RUM monitoring.'
  },
  cheat: ['Critical path: DOM + CSSOM, render tree, layout, paint, composite. CSS blocks render; scripts block parsing unless defer/async/module.', 'One main thread: keep tasks under 50 ms; animate transform and opacity; avoid layout thrashing.', 'CSR: simple but slow first paint. SSR: fast content, needs hydration. SSG/ISR: fastest, CDN. Islands/server components ship less JS.', 'Core Web Vitals: LCP under 2.5 s, INP under 200 ms, CLS under 0.1, measured at p75 with RUM.', 'Levers: less JS, optimized images and fonts, preload/preconnect, caching, virtualization, workers, reserved space.', 'Measure, find the bottleneck, fix, re-measure, enforce a budget.'],
  cards: [
    ['Name the steps of the critical rendering path.', 'DOM, CSSOM, render tree, layout, paint, composite.'],
    ['Why is CSS render-blocking and JS parser-blocking?', 'The browser avoids painting unstyled content, and scripts may modify the document being parsed.'],
    ['defer vs async?', 'Both download in parallel. defer runs after parsing, in order; async runs as soon as downloaded, in any order.'],
    ['Which CSS properties are cheap to animate?', 'transform and opacity (composite only).'],
    ['What is a long task?', 'Main-thread work over 50 ms that blocks input and rendering.'],
    ['CSR vs SSR vs SSG vs ISR?', 'CSR renders in the browser; SSR per request on server; SSG at build; ISR static pages regenerated in the background.'],
    ['What is hydration and its cost?', 'Attaching JS to server HTML to make it interactive; page looks ready but is unresponsive until done.'],
    ['What are the Core Web Vitals and good thresholds?', 'LCP under 2.5 s, INP under 200 ms, CLS under 0.1.'],
    ['Lab vs field data?', 'Lab (Lighthouse) is reproducible but synthetic; field/RUM reflects real users and devices.'],
    ['Why use keys in lists?', 'To identify items across renders so state and DOM attach to the right rows; use stable ids not indexes.'],
    ['Name three service worker caching strategies.', 'Cache-first, network-first, stale-while-revalidate.']
  ]
}
);
